---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { person } from '../data/person';

/* What AI DOGE is, in the terms its own method page uses
   (aidoge.co.uk/methodology/, read 29 September 2026). No coverage counts are
   repeated here: they change with every release, and the coverage page is the
   record. */
const schema = {
  '@type': 'WebApplication',
  '@id': 'https://aidoge.co.uk/#app',
  name: 'AI DOGE',
  url: 'https://aidoge.co.uk/',
  description: 'Searchable records of what public bodies in England publish about the money they spend, taken from the files those bodies publish themselves.',
  applicationCategory: 'Public spending data',
  operatingSystem: 'Web browser',
  isAccessibleForFree: true,
  inLanguage: 'en-GB',
  author: { '@id': person['@id'] },
  creator: { '@id': person['@id'] },
  subjectOf: { '@id': 'https://tompickup.co.uk/aidoge/' },
};
---

<Layout
  title="AI DOGE: public spending records | Tom Pickup"
  description="AI DOGE is an independent public spending transparency platform I built. It publishes what public bodies in England publish about their payments, from their own files, and does not estimate or rank."
  schema={schema}
>
  <section class="legal-page">
    <div class="container-narrow">
      <h1>AI DOGE</h1>
      <p class="legal-updated">Public spending records, from the files public bodies publish themselves</p>

      <div class="legal-content">
        <h2>What it is</h2>
        <p>
          AI DOGE is an independent public spending transparency platform I built. Councils in
          England are required to publish every payment they make above a threshold, usually
          &pound;500. AI DOGE collects those files, reads them into one common set of fields, and
          makes them searchable by public body and by supplier.
        </p>
        <p><a href="https://aidoge.co.uk/">Go to aidoge.co.uk</a></p>

        <h2>Where the figures come from</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Each body's own published payment files, taken as they are and read mechanically. No figure is retyped by hand.</li>
          <li>Budget and outturn figures from the returns councils submit to central government.</li>
          <li>Every page names the source it used and links to it.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How it is checked</h2>
        <p>
          Processed totals are reconciled back to the council's own published outturn return, or
          to its published budget where the outturn is not yet due. A mis-read column then shows
          up as a number that does not add up. A council year that does not reconcile shows no
          total at all.
        </p>

        <h2>What it will not do</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>It does not estimate. A figure that is not in a source document is left out.</li>
          <li>It does not rank councils by what they spend, because the periods held differ from council to council.</li>
          <li>It does not name individuals. Payments to individuals are counted in totals and never listed, and redactions in the published files are kept.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What spending data does not show</h2>
        <p>
          Payments above the threshold are not a council's whole budget. Staff pay, most small
          purchases and internal transfers are outside the published files. A total on AI DOGE is
          a total of what was published, not what the council spends.
        </p>

        <h2>Coverage and corrections</h2>
        <p>
          Coverage is uneven because publication is. The
          <a href="https://aidoge.co.uk/coverage/">coverage page</a> lists every body and what is
          held for it. If AI DOGE and a council's own file disagree, the council's file is right.
          Corrections are recorded on the
          <a href="https://aidoge.co.uk/methodology/#corrections">method page</a> with the
          original figure, the corrected figure and the date. Right of reply and complaints are
          set out in its <a href="https://aidoge.co.uk/policies/">policies</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>Used on this site</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/news/where-burnley-councils-money-goes/">Where Burnley Council&rsquo;s money goes</a></li>
          <li><a href="/news/temporary-accommodation-asylum-burnley/">Burnley&rsquo;s temporary accommodation bill</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
<style>
  .legal-page {
    padding: 7rem 0 var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .legal-page h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.75rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .legal-updated {
    font-size: 0.8125rem;
    color: var(--text-tertiary);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2xl);
  }

  .legal-content h2 {
    font-size: 1.125rem;
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--text-primary);
    margin-top: var(--space-xl);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-sm);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--separator);
  }

  .legal-content h2:first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .legal-content p {
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.75;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .legal-content ul {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-md);
  }

  .legal-content ul li {
    position: relative;
    padding-left: 20px;
    font-size: 0.9375rem;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    line-height: 1.75;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  .legal-content ul li::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 10px;
    width: 6px;
    height: 6px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    background: var(--accent);
    opacity: 0.6;
  }

  .legal-content a {
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .legal-content strong {
    color: var(--text-primary);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .legal-page {
      padding: 6rem 0 var(--space-xl);
    }
  }
</style>
